A game has n levels, connected by m teleporters, and your task is to get from level 1 to level n. The game has been designed so that there are no directed cycles in the underlying graph. In how many ways can you complete the game?

Input:
The first input line has two integers n and m: the number of levels and teleporters. The levels are numbered 1, 2, ..., n. After this, there are m lines describing the teleporters. Each line has two integers a and b: there is a teleporter from level a to level b.

Output:
Print one integer: the number of ways you can complete the game. Since the result may be large, print it modulo 10^9 + 7.

Constraints:
1 <= n <= 10^5
1 <= m <= 2 * 10^5
1 <= a, b <= n

Strategy:
This is topological sort but instead of finding one path and printing it we need to find the number of all paths. This is quite simple. We just do a normal topological sort but we keep a paths array and for each time we reach a node we add the paths of the parent node to the paths of the child. We use modulo there so there isnt overflow. This works because by adding the paths of the parent to the child we naturally combine all the parents of the node into it and that then propagates all the way to the end node. We output paths[n]. paths[1] get initialized to 1.